    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> count the Ramayana, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, among the Itihasas; it tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, held to be the seventh avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and portrays ideal sons, brothers, wives and kings.</li><li>Scholars place its oral composition roughly in the 8th to 4th centuries BCE, with later stages reaching the 3rd century CE; books two to six are the oldest.</li><li>It was retold in nearly every Indian language and in Buddhist and Jain versions, and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reamker">Reamker</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakawin">Kakawin</a> Ramayana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the Jain versions of the epic it is Lakshmana, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, who kills Ravana, and Jain texts hold that Ravana will become a future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

